<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 120 0 R
/T (Vorticella is a unicellular eukaryotic ciliate mostly found in freshwater habitats such as ponds, lakes, and slow-moving streams while usually being attached to plants. )
/E (Vorticella is a unicellular eukaryotic ciliate mostly found in freshwater habitats such as ponds, lakes, and slow-moving streams while usually being attached to plants. )
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
253 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 120 0 R
/T (The organism has a bell-shaped body mounted on a contractile stalk that anchors it to a surface. This stalk contains a contractile material called the spasmoneme, which allows Vorticella to coil rapidly in response to touch or sudden changes in the environment.)
/E (The organism has a bell-shaped body mounted on a contractile stalk that anchors it to a surface. This stalk contains a contractile material called the spasmoneme, which allows Vorticella to coil rapidly in response to touch or sudden changes in the environment.)
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
254 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 120 0 R
/T (Around the open end of the bell are rows of hair-like cilia arranged in a circular pattern. These cilia beat rapidly to create water currents that draw in bacteria and small organic particles, which are then directed into the cell mouth for feeding. Because of its clear and unique structure, feeding behavior, and rapid stalk contraction, Vorticella is frequently observed and is one of the most interesting ciliates. )
/E (Around the open end of the bell are rows of hair-like cilia arranged in a circular pattern. These cilia beat rapidly to create water currents that draw in bacteria and small organic particles, which are then directed into the cell mouth for feeding. Because of its clear and unique structure, feeding behavior, and rapid stalk contraction, Vorticella is frequently observed and is one of the most interesting ciliates. )
/Pg 14 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
